.he "peep (1)                     8/11/26                     peep (1)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
peep
- peephole optimizer


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
peep [-v] input.s output.s


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Peep
reads Z80 assembler, replaces short sequences with shorter ones that
do the same thing, and writes the result.  Both file arguments are
positional and both are required.

It works on what it can see in the text and nothing else: a window of
a few instructions at a time, matched against a table of patterns.  It
does not know the shape of the program, and it does not need to.  That
is deliberate - anything requiring a view of the whole function
belongs in
.bd
c1,
which has the tree in front of it.  What the two of them divide is
memory, not cleverness:
.bd
c1
is close enough to what the machine can hold that a rule which can be
found textually is better spent here.

The passes run over their own sources until nothing more changes.
This is not a small saving on a machine this size - it is 610 bytes
off
.bd
pass0,
1109 off
.bd
c0
and 1665 off
.bd
c1
- and the compiler is close enough to the ceiling that a few percent
is the difference between a source compiling on the target and not.

.bd
Peep
never changes what a program does.  A rule that fires has to leave the
flags and the registers the way the sequence it replaced left them,
and the cross-target gate compares what the compiler emits against the
host's on every source in the tree with the optimizer on.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-v	Report each rule as it fires, on standard error.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), c1 (1), asz (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
Errors are written to standard error and the exit status is non-zero.
Assembly it cannot parse is passed through unchanged rather than
rejected, so a construct it has not been taught still assembles.
